WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.330
Orbiting is an
installation piece that

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.620
is circled around a machine.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.990
And the machine creates floating
objects with a very particular

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.040
material.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.420
And these objects find a
particular choreography

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.380
in the space.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.415
And the idea is also to create
a very unreal atmosphere

00:00:28.415 --> 00:00:30.200
with that.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.180
No one has ever done it,
to make the machine that

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.310
produce a floating object.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.580
So that's, from the scientific
or engineering point of view,

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.290
it's really interesting.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.580
So right now, we're still
looking for a new material

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.670
that is very low density.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.520
We were trying everything we
could do in a very fun way.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.290
So that's why I think we found
a material from a very daily

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.200
object like a glass or bubble.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.850
It may take like
three years more.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.380
But as long as we have this
final goal to be realized,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.300
I think this process, every
step, is really meaningful.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.000
Hiroshi Ishii is
a scientist that

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.920
has a very artistic
approach towards processes

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.460
of production,
processes of research.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.090
He has a very good idea of
the aesthetical outcome of it.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.520
And also in this work, with
the Tangible Media Group,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.760
I think there is a clear
awareness that aesthetics

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.580
add some kind of
meaning to an object

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.350
or to a machine that goes
beyond the pure functionality.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.150
Well, artistically, Thom
projects a lot of the meaning.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.550
He wants to make
many object which

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.510
represent cultural symbols
floating in the air,

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.240
circulating, and then disappear.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.610
Process is very ephemeral.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.870
And usually, we have a final
artifacts, like objects.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:02.000
Like scientific work, published
paper is a final product.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.970
But all the process, struggle,
discussion, prototyping,

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:08.660
failing, laughing, and
crying, never be captured.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.460
But that has a real essence
of the collaboration,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.800
with joy, also value.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.920
If you work with many people
who have different perspective,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.770
you can really expand your
scope and the boundaries.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.330
As an engineer and a scientist,
that really piqued my interest

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.310
because I feel
like in art you can

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.610
ask these sorts of questions
that aren't practical

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.480
and that maybe have no
immediate function or utility,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.290
but ultimately push the
bounds of what we can think of

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.350
and what we can do.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.850
If you want to make
stuff float, you

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:49.530
need a very, very thin,
pliable, elastic material

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.990
that you can kind of blow
up and maximize the surface

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.000
area to volume ratio.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.490
Thom, for this orbiting project,
wanted to do the opposite.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.630
He wanted a material
that was rigid,

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.970
that could be
molded into a shape.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.850
And then right as it's molded
and inflated with helium,

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.980
could just float away off
of a fabrication machine.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.260
And here, it was really
just a simple question-- can

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.020
you make stuff float that
takes on new geometries

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.160
and has new properties?

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.220
And what is the fabrication
technology you need to do that?

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.070
All the stuff
floating in the air,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.640
it's something
very, very strongly

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.290
imprinted in our brain.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.550
So even if the project
does not reach that point,

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:32.480
I hope we can keep dreaming
the which we can really

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.630
control the gravity
that make things

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.720
float in the air at our will.

